54,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
55,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Looking at the candidates, the Integrated Fare Platform seems the least taxing on our depleted rosters, provided the technology is stable.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Takumi,,I disagree. The journey audits show ticketing isn't the bottleneck. The Crosstown Bus Corridor addresses the actual transit time delays I found.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The Bus Corridor involves paving, and my data shows paving rates are climbing. The Fare Platform might be cheaper if we can move away from multiple contractors.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Servas,,"The Night Service Extension aligns with the successful pilot I found. However, @Asuka's concerns about the late-turn pool make it very risky.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Station Access Upgrades are a nightmare given the pre-war shell voids. The Bus Corridor is safer, but the Fare Platform avoids the physical ground entirely.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, the Bus Corridor requires dedicated lanes and boarding islands. Who builds and maintains them? Our roadway crews are already below establishment.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, it's a necessary risk to fix the transit times. But if the crews are that low, perhaps the Fare Platform is the only ""safe"" bet.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,The Fare Platform replaces three separate contractors. That could potentially stop the bleed in our capital ledger. It's the most logical financial move.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Servas,,"If we cannot extend the night service due to staffing, and the stations are too risky, the Fare Platform or the Bus Corridor remain.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Servas, the Fare Platform requires the ferry and rail offices to agree. My findings show they can't even agree on data terms. It might stall indefinitely.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, that is a critical point. If the Fare Platform is a political deadlock, the Bus Corridor is the only one left, despite the crew shortage.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"If the Fare Platform is stalled by bureaucracy, then the Bus Corridor is the only way to actually improve the passenger's journey time.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"I dislike the paving costs, but a project that actually gets built is better than a ""lean"" one that never starts due to committee arguments.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Servas,,"The Bus Corridor's lane-priority stretches have failed in the past, but the need for mid-city efficiency is clear. It is a pragmatic compromise.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,It avoids the pre-war shells and the inter-agency data wars. It is the most likely to actually move forward.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Asuka,,I can support the Bus Corridor if we prioritize the boarding islands over complex roadworks to save our crews. I vote for B.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Takumi,,It fixes the actual time loss for the passengers. I vote for B.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"Despite the paving rates, it's the most viable path that avoids the technology budget cuts. I vote for B.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Servas,,It is a balanced choice that addresses current needs while avoiding the staffing crisis of the night extension. I vote for B.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"It is the only plan that doesn't rely on the ""unrecorded voids"" or the joint committee's cooperation. I vote for B.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
